    <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start;">
        <div style="width: 50%;">
            <h2 class="section-title">The benefits</h2>
            <h3 style="font-size: 18pt; margin-top: 10px;">Microprocessor technology</h3>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>Royce Thompson utilise microprocessor technology with specifically designed photocell control software.</p>
                <p>The microprocessor with control circuitry includes sophisticated technology for voltage supply, temperature, synchronous switching and advanced control algorithms, allowing for a wide range of load switching reliability across to zero point switching.</p>
                <p>Fundamentally, the state machine ensures exact control of the lighting unit providing an instant switch ON when the specified light level is reached, and an instant switch OFF when the light level drops below the threshold.</p>
                <p>Microprocessor technology in Royce Thompson photocells delivers significant benefits:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li>Greater load handling capacity - up to <span data-field="load_capacity">{{ load_capacity }}</span></li>
                    <li>No contact arcing</li>
                    <li>Positive/negative switching differential (1.1.5 positive, 1.0.5 negative or 1.0/0.9 differential)</li>
                    <li>Instant switch ON/OFF</li>
                    <li>Inverted use, close to a Sodium light source</li>
                    <li>Very low power consumption for clear energy savings (e.g. 0.25 W vs 0.3 W)</li>
                </ul>
                <p>For the vast majority of applications, such as street and amenity lighting, Royce Thompson network via an unmetered supply, without their energy consumption being recorded.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div style="width: 48%;">
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>In order to confirm their energy consumption, photocells must be tested, inventoried and provided with a charge code by the relevant authority for unmetered equipment (ELE/UNS/USG) - the Unmetered Supplies User Group.</p>
                <p>The charge code defines the circuit Watts of the equipment and confirms that testing has been undertaken by the manufacturer, thus ensuring energy consumption has been recorded as accurately as possible.</p>
                <h3 style="font-size: 16pt; margin-top: 30px;">State Machine Pre-programmed Lux Levels</h3>
                <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-around; align-items: center; margin-top: 20px;">
                    <div style="text-align: center;">
                        <img src="placeholder_lux_diagram_1.png" alt="Lux Diagram 1" style="width: 150px; height: auto;">
                        <p class="caption">Dawn 5-240 LUX</p>
                    </div>
                    <div style="text-align: center;">
                        <img src="placeholder_lux_diagram_2.png" alt="Lux Diagram 2" style="width: 150px; height: auto;">
                        <p class="caption">Night 2 LUX</p>
                    </div>
                    <div style="text-align: center;">
                        <img src="placeholder_lux_diagram_3.png" alt="Lux Diagram 3" style="width: 150px; height: auto;">
                        <p class="caption">Day 250 LUX +</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <p style="margin-top: 30px;">Photocell energy consumption charges are based on the average power consumption over a 24 hour period and the control gear used, and the photocell switch setting.</p>
                <p>Selecting the most appropriate photocell and switch setting can therefore deliver significant energy and cost savings being (see overview).</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

